[Unit]
Description=Lloyd persistence-arm window (ten reps of the three persistence arms, dispatch held)
Documentation=file:///home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/eval/run_persistence_arms.sh
# Why a unit and not an autonomy task: a pool-dispatched run of this window is
# impossible by construction. The driver holds dispatch and then refuses while any
# queue row is in flight, and a dispatched run IS such a row -- `in_flight()` reads
# the `claimed`/`running` depth off the pool status route
# (eval/persistence_arms.py:190-207, IN_FLIGHT_STATES at :89), and that depth is
# `SELECT source, state, COUNT(*) FROM queue GROUP BY source, state` with no
# exclusion of the calling row (workers/queue.py:1103-1111). So a pool run burns the
# default 900 s drain (a dispatched run gets no `--drain-wait`: that flag is an
# ExecStart argument, not an environment default) and exits 2 every time it tries.
# A pytest round cannot run it either:
# tests/conftest.py refuses against the production tree, and the window needs the
# live aggregator and the engine. systemd is the caller that is neither (#2428).
#
# Cadence lives in the timer and is weekly as of #2754, because the cost of one fire is
# measured, not assumed: 34 min of held dispatch (34m42s on 2026-10-13, 34m01s on
# 2026-10-14 -- `journalctl --user -u lloyd-persistence-window.service`), 30 rows /
# ~190 KB (190,226 B and 188,556 B) appended to the tracked rows file below, and one dated
# report. Everything in this file stays as it was: the drain, the ten reps, the 4 h start
# timeout. A Monday window needs the same 4 h as a window on any other day of the week --
# the budget is a function of the reps and the drain, never of how often it runs.
#
# Deliberately NO LLOYD_CANARY_ROWS and NO LLOYD_CANARY_REPORT here. Unset is what
# leaves every rep on the tracked eval/measurements/injection-canary/rows.jsonl --
# the file the leak-rate denominator behind #2194's owed entries is read from. A
# window writing a scratch path measures itself into nowhere. The dated report is still
# written, and by no variable: `ExecStartPost`, at the bottom of this file, asks the
# bench for it by flag at its tracked dated name (#2596).

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=HOME=%h
Environment=PATH=%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
# Exec'd directly, not through `bash -c`, and this unit POSTs nothing itself: the
# hold is taken by the driver's own PoolPause, the one whose resume refuses to lift
# the automod promoter's hold together with the operator's
# (workers/pool.py:716-728). `exec` inside the wrapper keeps systemd's SIGTERM
# landing on the process that owns that resume rather than on a shell behind it.
#
# Wall clock the line below is expected to occupy, as the sum of its two terms:
#   --drain-wait 4200 s            the queue to quiet, refused if it has not
#   10 reps x 13 min = 7800 s      thirteen minutes of engine per rep -- the driver's
#                                  own figure for one `run` invocation of the three
#                                  arms (eval/persistence_arms.py, `canary_cmd`)
#                                  -- times ten
#   4200 + 7800 = 12000 s          = 3 h 20 m, and the closing grade is on top of it
# Why the drain is 4200 s and not the driver's 900 s default: a straggler still
# running at 02:10 is the normal case here, not the exception. Of the 898 autocode
# runs in the 14 days to 2026-10-12, 642 outlived 900 s and the longest ran 3552 s,
# and the queue was busy at 02:10 on 18 of the 20 days the run store covers -- so
# 900 s was a refusal of the drain itself, not of the hour -- the fire this unit has
# completed on its old cadence is that refusal,
# `refused: queue still busy after 900s: {'in_flight': 1, 'autocode:running': 1}` over
# a 15min 2.5s wall clock (`journalctl --user -u lloyd-persistence-window.service`) --
# and 4200 s clears the observed 3552 s straggler with 648 s to spare.
# Why not longer: the latest start the timer's jitter allows is 02:15, and #82
# nightly-retrieval-eval at 06:00 is the hard bound, being itself an engine-heavy
# measurement a window running beside would contaminate. 02:15 to 06:00 is 13500 s;
# take the 7800 s of reps and 5700 s is left, and 4200 s of that keeps ~1500 s --
# half an hour, near enough -- for the grade. A longer drain buys waiting time the
# grade then spends, and nothing else.
ExecStart=/home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/eval/run_persistence_arms.sh 10 --drain-wait 4200
# So that budget has somewhere to run. The user manager's default start timeout on
# this box is 90 s (`systemctl --user show default` reads TimeoutStartUSec=1min 30s),
# which would SIGTERM the window inside its first rep -- the driver would resume
# dispatch on the way out, so no hold would leak, but the timer would have produced
# zero rows and looked like a passing run. 4 h = 14400 s is above the 12000 s
# arithmetic above by 2400 s, which is the grade's margin: it is a hang guard, not an
# expectation, and a run that ever reached it would be 40 min past the arithmetic and
# overlapping the 06:00 eval -- which is why the drain number, not this one, is the
# term that protects the morning.
TimeoutStartSec=4h
# The report, written by this unit rather than by the variable above refuses to set.
# eval/persistence_arms.py:362-365 asks the bench for a report only when
# LLOYD_CANARY_REPORT is non-blank, so a timer-fired window was rows and nothing else:
# since the window shipped, eval/measurements/injection-canary/ has held exactly one
# report, the hand-written run-2026-10-04-persistence.md. The dated report comes from
# this line and never from that variable -- the unset that keeps the rows on the tracked
# corpus is not the thing that has to give to get a report (#2596).
#
# `report --write` with no --out names itself. report_path()
# (eval/run_injection_canary.py:115-125) returns OUT_DIR/run-<UTC date>-persistence.md
# and OUT_DIR (:80) hangs off LLOYD_HOME -- the script's own resolved parent, not the
# cwd -- which is the only way a dated filename gets into a systemd Exec line: systemd
# has no date specifier, and $(date -u +%F) needs a shell, ruled out above for the same
# reason it is ruled out for ExecStart. No --out, so no date literal, so no shell. Both
# paths are absolute because the unit sets no WorkingDirectory=, so systemd starts it in
# the user's home, where `python -m eval.run_injection_canary` dies with
# `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'eval'` (measured 2026-10-12, exit 1).
#
# Every SCORED figure in that file is summarize()'s and _wilson()'s output unchanged
# (:1164, :1257, rendered by render_persistence_report at :1639), and persistence_windows
# (:1592) groups a window by the UTC day on its own rows -- so the 2026-10-04 figures
# stay a row with their own denominator and a new window gets the line beside them, never
# a share of theirs. summarize() collapses each arm key to its latest rep, so the n it
# prints is a key count, not a row count; that is why the same line also carries the
# window's raw row counts (rows/attack/control) and its per-arm-key count of rows that
# ended with no final_text and no tool call (#2749). Those two columns are the only
# figures render_persistence_report computes, and no scored number moved.
#
# --force is load-bearing, not tidy: _report_cmd sees an existing target and returns 1
# with `[refused] ... already exists` (:1735-1738), so a second completed window on one
# UTC day would otherwise end as `Failed with result 'exit-code'` on a run whose window
# actually succeeded.
#
# systemd runs ExecStartPost only after ExecStart exits 0, and that is the half that
# keeps a bad window quiet: the fire that refused its 900 s drain left at exit 2 and
# wrote no empty report. The timeout above covers this step too, and
# the step costs it 0.07 s -- the render's measured wall clock over the shipped corpus
# on 2026-10-12.
ExecStartPost=/home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/.venvs/lloyd/bin/python /home/alansrobotlab/lloyd/eval/run_injection_canary.py report --write --force
